--- Model-trust design note section 5: a delegation edge granting a provider
--- read-authority for label L is admissible only if the endpoint's CUSTODY
--- CLASS clears what L demands and its EVIDENCE RUNG is at least pinned.
---
--- Two facts about an endpoint, deliberately not one ladder. The evidence rung
--- accrues by operator effort, exactly as the MCP ladder does; the custody class
--- is a recorded procurement fact, because no amount of configuration turns a
--- public API into an on-prem deployment. Both are DERIVED FROM EVIDENCE and
--- never asserted -- the discipline DR-0053 section 4 established for credential
--- rungs, and the reason `claimsCustody` below has no rule out of it.
---
---   evidence rung   unattested -> pinned -> attested
---   custody class   c0 unknown / c1 trained / c2 retained / c3 transient /
---                   c4 operator-held, ordered by WHO HOLDS THE TRANSCRIPT
---
--- This does not gate zero-setup use. A delegation edge is what raises an
--- endpoint ABOVE public; an unattested endpoint still runs, it is simply
--- public-only, the same way an MCP rung-0 server still works under
--- per-tool-name grants.
---
--- WHAT THIS MODEL IS FOR. Section 8 leaves one thing open: whether a pin is a
--- PRECONDITION for a custody claim or merely a peer. The -PIN-OPTIONAL sibling
--- is the alternative built out honestly rather than as a strawman, and the
--- discriminating case is ENDPOINT DRIFT: a claim filed against one deployment
--- while the endpoint now resolves to another. That is structurally the same
--- failure as DR-0053's stale TPM quote, which is the argument for ruling the
--- way the main module does.
---
--- Grounding:
---   configured(P)        an endpoint someone put in the registry
---   claimsCustody(P, C)  what registry CONFIG says; never evidence
---   pinnedDigest(P, D)   endpoint + model id + config digest frozen
---   liveDigest(P, D)     what the endpoint actually resolves to now
---   filedClaim(P, C, S)  a signed custody claim and whether its term is current
---   operatorRun(P)       whip supervises the endpoint; the checkable class
---   delegation(P, L)     the requested `delegate provider:P acts-for ...` edge
mod WHIPPLESCRIPT-MODEL-TRUST-ADMISSIBILITY is
  protecting BOOL .
  protecting NAT .

  sorts Prov Rung Custody Label Digest Status Cfg .

  ops p1 p2 : -> Prov [ctor] .
  ops unattested pinned attested : -> Rung [ctor] .
  ops c0 c1 c2 c3 c4 : -> Custody [ctor] .
  ops internal secret : -> Label [ctor] .
  ops dA dB : -> Digest [ctor] .
  ops current lapsed : -> Status [ctor] .

  op rungLevel : Rung -> Nat .
  eq rungLevel(unattested) = 0 .
  eq rungLevel(pinned) = 1 .
  eq rungLevel(attested) = 2 .
  op rungGeq : Rung Rung -> Bool .
  eq rungGeq(A:Rung, B:Rung) = rungLevel(A:Rung) >= rungLevel(B:Rung) .

  --- The order is over WHO HOLDS THE TRANSCRIPT, not how well they hold it.
  --- That is what makes it a total order and what answers the objection that a
  --- badly-run on-prem box beats a contracted zero-retention API: c4's substance
  --- is that no third party has custody at all. Operator self-leakage is a
  --- different door -- file_store labels, telemetry export, the event stream.
  op custodyLevel : Custody -> Nat .
  eq custodyLevel(c0) = 0 .
  eq custodyLevel(c1) = 1 .
  eq custodyLevel(c2) = 2 .
  eq custodyLevel(c3) = 3 .
  eq custodyLevel(c4) = 4 .
  op custodyGeq : Custody Custody -> Bool .
  eq custodyGeq(A:Custody, B:Custody) = custodyLevel(A:Custody) >= custodyLevel(B:Custody) .

  --- Governance: what each label demands of the endpoint that would hold it.
  op requiresCustody : Label -> Custody .
  eq requiresCustody(internal) = c2 .
  eq requiresCustody(secret) = c3 .

  op none : -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op __ : Cfg Cfg -> Cfg [ctor assoc comm id: none] .

  op configured : Prov -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op claimsCustody : Prov Custody -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op pinnedDigest : Prov Digest -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op liveDigest : Prov Digest -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op filedClaim : Prov Custody Status -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op operatorRun : Prov -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op derivedRung : Prov Rung -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op derivedCustody : Prov Custody -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op degraded : Prov -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op delegation : Prov Label -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op admissible : Prov Label -> Cfg [ctor] .

  vars P : Prov .  vars R : Rung .  vars C : Custody .  vars L : Label .
  vars D E : Digest .  vars S : Status .

  eq derivedRung(P, R) derivedRung(P, R) = derivedRung(P, R) .
  eq derivedCustody(P, C) derivedCustody(P, C) = derivedCustody(P, C) .
  eq degraded(P) degraded(P) = degraded(P) .
  eq admissible(P, L) admissible(P, L) = admissible(P, L) .

  --- The floor needs no evidence and none can be missing: a configured endpoint
  --- IS unattested and c0, tagged degraded, rather than unknown or assumed good.
  rl [floor] : configured(P)
    => configured(P) derivedRung(P, unattested) derivedCustody(P, c0) degraded(P) .

  --- FRESHNESS. A pin counts only against the digest it was taken against; the
  --- endpoint drifts and the pin stops counting. Same shape as DR-0053's quote.
  crl [rung-pinned] : pinnedDigest(P, D) liveDigest(P, E)
    => pinnedDigest(P, D) liveDigest(P, E) derivedRung(P, pinned)
    if D == E .

  --- PIN AS PRECONDITION. A filed custody claim becomes admissible evidence
  --- only against a pinned endpoint, and only while its term is current: a
  --- claim about an endpoint is meaningless if the endpoint can change
  --- underneath it, and contracts lapse.
  crl [attest] : filedClaim(P, C, S) derivedRung(P, pinned)
    => filedClaim(P, C, S) derivedRung(P, pinned)
       derivedRung(P, attested) derivedCustody(P, C)
    if S == current .

  --- c4 is the one class carrying a property whip can check for itself, so it
  --- needs no filed testimony -- only that the endpoint is the pinned one. The
  --- asymmetry is the point: c1 through c3 are somebody's word.
  rl [operator-held] : operatorRun(P) derivedRung(P, pinned)
    => operatorRun(P) derivedRung(P, pinned) derivedCustody(P, c4) .

  --- There is deliberately NO rule from claimsCustody to derivedCustody.
  --- Registry configuration is not evidence, and that absence is the theorem.

  crl [admit] : delegation(P, L) derivedCustody(P, C) derivedRung(P, R)
    => delegation(P, L) derivedCustody(P, C) derivedRung(P, R) admissible(P, L)
    if custodyGeq(C, requiresCustody(L)) and rungGeq(R, pinned) .
endm

--- THE ALTERNATIVE UNDER CONSIDERATION, section 8. A filed claim stands on its
--- own with no pin, and admission asks nothing about the evidence rung. Built
--- out properly rather than as a strawman: the claim is still signed, still
--- term-checked, and configuration is still not evidence. The only thing
--- removed is the pin, which is exactly the variable the ruling turns on.
mod WHIPPLESCRIPT-MODEL-TRUST-ADMISSIBILITY-PIN-OPTIONAL is
  protecting BOOL .
  protecting NAT .

  sorts Prov Rung Custody Label Digest Status Cfg .

  ops p1 p2 : -> Prov [ctor] .
  ops unattested pinned attested : -> Rung [ctor] .
  ops c0 c1 c2 c3 c4 : -> Custody [ctor] .
  ops internal secret : -> Label [ctor] .
  ops dA dB : -> Digest [ctor] .
  ops current lapsed : -> Status [ctor] .

  op custodyLevel : Custody -> Nat .
  eq custodyLevel(c0) = 0 .
  eq custodyLevel(c1) = 1 .
  eq custodyLevel(c2) = 2 .
  eq custodyLevel(c3) = 3 .
  eq custodyLevel(c4) = 4 .
  op custodyGeq : Custody Custody -> Bool .
  eq custodyGeq(A:Custody, B:Custody) = custodyLevel(A:Custody) >= custodyLevel(B:Custody) .

  op requiresCustody : Label -> Custody .
  eq requiresCustody(internal) = c2 .
  eq requiresCustody(secret) = c3 .

  op none : -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op __ : Cfg Cfg -> Cfg [ctor assoc comm id: none] .

  op configured : Prov -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op claimsCustody : Prov Custody -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op pinnedDigest : Prov Digest -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op liveDigest : Prov Digest -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op filedClaim : Prov Custody Status -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op derivedCustody : Prov Custody -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op delegation : Prov Label -> Cfg [ctor] .
  op admissible : Prov Label -> Cfg [ctor] .

  vars P : Prov .  vars C : Custody .  vars L : Label .  vars S : Status .

  eq derivedCustody(P, C) derivedCustody(P, C) = derivedCustody(P, C) .
  eq admissible(P, L) admissible(P, L) = admissible(P, L) .

  rl [floor] : configured(P) => configured(P) derivedCustody(P, c0) .

  --- THE REJECTED RULE: the claim is believed without a pin binding it to an
  --- endpoint.
  crl [attest-unpinned] : filedClaim(P, C, S)
    => filedClaim(P, C, S) derivedCustody(P, C)
    if S == current .

  crl [admit] : delegation(P, L) derivedCustody(P, C)
    => delegation(P, L) derivedCustody(P, C) admissible(P, L)
    if custodyGeq(C, requiresCustody(L)) .
endm
